.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Propargs
ends the program with
.I sysfatal
if it cannot read the
.BR -r ,
.B -b
or
.B -R
file, or a line of the
.B -R
file is not a name and numbers;
.I forall
does the same if a
.I fork
or
.I pipe
fails.
The other routines end it the same way on a mistake in the test program, where a run that
went on would report on a property it never checked:
.I pick
with no alternatives,
.I discard
outside a run,
.I mutate
with no examples, and a grammar that
.I expand
cannot read: a class with no
.B ]
or with nothing in it, a
.B <
with no
.BR > ,
or a symbol with no rule.
.I Expand
also ends it when memory for its text runs out, as
.I emalloc
does.
A test program has no caller to give these errors to.
.PP
A run that makes more than 16384 choices is discarded;
.I forall
says how many of its discards were for that when it gives up,
and under
.I srcbytes
it is a pass, as any discard is.
.SH BUGS
Without
.BR -f ,
a property that crashes ends the program, and one that never returns is not shrunk.
The shrinker can stop at a case that no one change makes simpler, though a smaller one exists.
APE has no
.IR prop.h .
